# aeneid-009 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Equus ligneus. Sinon"
TITLE_EN = "The Wooden Horse and Sinon — Aeneid II.1–104"
PART = "The Wooden Horse and Sinon (II.1–104)"
ABOUT = "Book II opens in the hall at Carthage. Everyone falls silent, and Aeneas, from his high couch, begins the story Dido asked for. He calls it an unspeakable grief, one he shared in, and says that even a Greek soldier could hardly tell it dry-eyed; but if she is so eager to hear Troy's last agony, he will begin, though his mind shrinks from the memory. Worn down by the war and by the fates, the Greek leaders, after so many years, build a horse as big as a hill, plank it with cut fir, and say it is a vow for their safe return. In its dark flank they shut picked men and fill its belly with armed soldiers. Then the fleet sails out of sight and hides behind the island of Tenedos. The Trojans, thinking the Greeks gone home, pour out of the gates and wander through the empty camp. They argue about the horse: Thymoetes would drag it inside the walls; Capys and the wiser heads would drown it or burn it or cut it open. Laocoön runs down from the citadel, warns them that the Greeks are not to be trusted, whatever the horse is, and hurls a spear into its side; the hollow belly groans, and only the fates, Aeneas says, kept the Trojans from tearing the hiding place open. Then Trojan shepherds drag in a bound young man, who has let himself be taken on purpose. The crowd closes round him to jeer, and Aeneas tells Dido to learn from this one man what the Greeks are. Standing before them, the prisoner cries out that neither land nor sea will have him, the Greeks having cast him off and the Trojans wanting his blood. The Trojans pity him and urge him to say who he is. He gives his name as Sinon and makes a long speech, which fills the rest of this part: he will tell the whole truth, he says, and admits he is a Greek. He tells of Palamedes, an innocent man whom the Greeks put to death, and of his own father, who sent him to serve Palamedes as a boy; of Ulysses, who pursued him once Palamedes was dead; and then breaks off, asking why he bothers to tell them this, and bids them take their vengeance at once, for Ulysses and the sons of Atreus would pay well for it."
U = [
((1, 'tenebant.'),
"They fell silent, all, and, intent, held their faces fixed.",
"All fell silent, and they kept their faces turned toward him, intent.",
"A new paragraph, and a new book; Book II is the narrative Aeneas gives at Dido's request at the end of Book I, so Virgil's own voice steps back and a character speaks for nearly the whole book. 'Conticuere' = 'conticuerunt' (the -ere ending for -erunt; you'll see this constantly). 'intenti' is nominative plural, agreeing with the unexpressed subject 'omnes'. 'ora tenebant' — 'held their faces', that is, kept them turned toward the speaker; 'tenebant' is imperfect, so the silence lasts."),
((2, 'alto:'),
"Then from the couch father Aeneas thus began from on high:",
"Then father Aeneas began, from the high couch where he lay:",
"'toro… ab alto' — the guests reclined on couches; Aeneas speaks from the raised one (the guests lay on couches at the feast of Book I). 'pater' is Aeneas's regular title in the poem: a father, and the head of his people. 'orsus' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'ordior' ('begin', used of speech or weaving), a passive form with active sense; the sentence has no verb of saying, only 'sic orsus', with the verb ('est') dropped. The colon opens his speech; Greenough prints no speech mark here."),
((5, 'Danai;'),
"An unspeakable grief, queen, you bid me renew,\nhow the Trojan wealth and the lamentable kingdom\nthe Danaans overthrew;",
"Queen, you bid me live again a grief too great for words: how the Greeks tore down Troy's wealth and her kingdom, so worthy of tears;",
"'Infandum… dolorem' — 'un-speakable', from 'for, fari' (to speak); the opening word hangs over the whole book. 'renovare' — to make new again, to relive. 'ut… eruerint' — an indirect question depending on 'dolorem renovare' (perfect subjunctive, 'how they have overthrown'). 'Troianas opes' is wealth, resources, power; 'lamentabile' is 'worthy of lament'. 'Danai' (nominative plural) is one of the commonest names for the Greeks in the poem, after Danaus; 'Argivi', 'Achivi' and 'Pelasgi' are others, all used interchangeably."),
((6, 'fui.'),
"and the most wretched things which I myself saw,\nand in which a great part I was.",
"and the most pitiful sights I saw myself, in which I played a great part.",
"'quaeque' = 'et quae', 'and the things which': it joins a second object to 'renovare' ('to renew the grief of how… and of the things which…'). 'ipse' (I myself) claims eyewitness authority: the whole of Book II is a firsthand account. 'miserrima' is a superlative, 'most wretched'. 'quorum pars magna fui' — 'of which I was a great part': a relative clause with 'pars' as predicate; the phrase was famous in Latin."),
((8, 'lacrimis?'),
"Who, such things in telling, of the Myrmidons\nor Dolopes, or soldier of hard Ulysses,\nwould keep from tears?",
"Who, in telling such things, would hold back his tears, though he were a Myrmidon or a Dolopian, or a soldier of stern Ulysses?",
"A rhetorical question. 'Quis… temperet' — present subjunctive of potential or deliberation ('who would keep himself...?'), with 'a lacrimis' ('from tears'; 'temperare' takes 'a' + ablative of what one refrains from). 'talia fando' is an ablative of the gerund, 'in telling such things'. 'Myrmidonum', 'Dolopum' (here with -ve, 'or') are old genitive plurals in -um (you'll see this constantly): the Myrmidons were Achilles's men, the Dolopes were a Thessalian people who also fought for the Greeks. 'duri… Ulixi' — 'Ulixi' is the old genitive of 'Ulixes'; Ulysses is 'hard' for his cunning. The point: even Greeks would weep."),
((9, 'somnos.'),
"And already damp night from the sky\nis rushing down, and the setting stars persuade to sleep.",
"And already dewy night is falling from the sky, and the sinking stars invite us to sleep.",
"'Et iam' marks the lateness of the hour; 'nox umida' — night is 'moist' with dew. 'praecipitat' is intransitive here, 'plunges', a favourite verb for the setting of night or the sun. 'suadent' — 'urge, counsel' with the accusative 'somnos'. The stars 'falling' (cadentia) are the stars setting; the line suggests Aeneas could leave off, which makes his decision to go on, in the next sentence, an act of courtesy."),
((13, 'incipiam.'),
"But if so great is the love to know our misfortunes\nand briefly to hear Troy's last labour,\nthough my mind shrinks to remember, and flees back in grief,\nI will begin.",
"But if you so much want to know our misfortunes and hear, in brief, of Troy's last ordeal, then — though my mind shudders to recall it and recoils in grief — I will begin.",
"A long conditional period: 'si tantus amor (est)… incipiam', with the 'quamquam' clause (concession, indicative) inside it. 'casus cognoscere nostros' and 'audire' are infinitives after 'amor' (a noun that takes an infinitive, 'the desire to know'). 'supremum… laborem' — 'Troy's final ordeal'. 'meminisse' is a perfect infinitive with present meaning ('to remember'). 'animus horret… refugit' — the mind bristles and flees back, a physical image. The verb comes last: 'incipiam' is the whole delay resolved at once. In Greenough's text the full stop is printed with no space after it ('incipiam.Fracti'), so the unit stops at 'incipiam.'; the next sentence begins mid-line."),
((16, 'costas:'),
"Broken by war and driven back by the fates,\nthe leaders of the Danaans, so many years now gliding by,\nbuild a horse the size of a mountain by the divine art of Pallas,\nand weave its ribs of cut fir:",
"Broken by the war and driven back by the fates, the leaders of the Greeks, as the years slipped by, build a horse the size of a hill, with Pallas's divine skill, and weave its ribs of sawn fir:",
"'Fracti… repulsi' — two perfect participles, 'broken' and 'repelled', in the nominative with 'ductores'. 'Danaum' is the old genitive plural (= 'Danaorum'). 'tot iam labentibus annis' — ablative absolute, 'so many years now gliding past'. 'instar montis' — 'the very image (of a mountain)', a noun in apposition with 'equum', 'as big as a hill'. 'divina Palladis arte' — Pallas Athena is the divine craftswoman behind the horse. 'intexunt' — 'weave in' (like a loom); the planks are fitted in. 'secta abiete' — fir (a ship-timber), cut."),
((17, 'simulant;'),
"a vow for their return they pretend;",
"they give it out as a vow for their safe return;",
"'votum pro reditu' — an offering for a safe homecoming. 'simulant' — 'pretend', the first hint of the lie on which the whole story turns; Aeneas gives it away at once, looking back."),
((17, 'vagatur.'),
"that report goes wandering.",
"and that is the story that spreads.",
"'ea fama vagatur' — the rumour 'wanders' (a standard verb for rumour moving about). 'ea' agrees with 'fama'. The Trojans' mistake will be to believe it, but Aeneas does not say so yet."),
((20, 'complent.'),
"Here, chosen bodies of men, drawn by lot, in secret\nthey shut into the dark flank, and deep the caverns\nvast and the womb they fill with armed soldiery.",
"Into its dark side, in secrecy, they shut picked men chosen by lot, and fill its huge caverns and its very womb with armed soldiery.",
"'Huc' — 'into this (horse)'. 'delecta virum… corpora' — 'chosen bodies of men'; 'virum' is the old genitive plural, 'virorum'. 'sortiti' is a deponent perfect participle (active in sense), 'having drawn lots'. 'caeco lateri' — dative of 'includere', the flank is 'blind' (dark). 'cavernas ingentis' — 'ingentis' is an accusative plural in -is (Greenough keeps these, for 'ingentes'). 'penitus' — 'deep inside'. 'uterum' — 'the womb', 'belly', a bold metaphor Virgil uses repeatedly of the horse: the Greeks are an unborn brood. 'armato milite' — ablative of material, 'with armed soldiery' (a collective singular)."),
((23, 'carinis:'),
"There is in sight Tenedos, most famous by fame,\nan island, rich in wealth while Priam's kingdoms remained,\nnow only a bay and an anchorage poorly faithful to ships:",
"In view lies Tenedos, an island very well known by repute, rich in resources while Priam's kingdom stood, now only a bay, and a roadstead that cannot be trusted by ships:",
"A new paragraph. Tenedos is a small island just off the Trojan coast, visible from the plain. 'notissima fama' — 'best known by report'. 'dum… manebant' — 'dum' with the imperfect indicative, 'as long as', 'while' (here plural 'regna' for the one kingdom, a poetic plural). 'nunc tantum' — 'now only'. 'statio male fida carinis' — 'an anchorage unfaithful to keels', that is, unsafe for ships (an open roadstead with no shelter). The description is of Tenedos as Aeneas knows it now, after the fall; the present tense is the speaker's present."),
((24, 'condunt.'),
"hither, drawn out, on the deserted shore they hide themselves.",
"Here they sail out and hide themselves on its empty shore.",
"'huc' — 'to this place'. 'provecti' is a perfect participle of 'provehor' (passive in form, 'carried forward, sailed out'), with active sense here. 'deserto in litore' — the shore is empty of people, so they can hide. 'se condunt' — 'hide themselves'."),
((25, 'Mycenas:'),
"We, believing them gone, and by the wind to have sought Mycenae:",
"We thought they had gone, and that the wind had taken them home to Mycenae.",
"'Nos abiisse rati' — an accusative-and-infinitive after 'rati' (deponent perfect participle of 'reor', 'having thought'), with 'eos' (the Greeks) left to be supplied; 'abiisse' and 'petiisse' are perfect infinitives (the second a contraction of 'petivisse'), and the sentence has no main verb of its own. Mycenae is Agamemnon's city, standing for Greece. The colon leads into the Trojans' reaction."),
((26, 'luctu;'),
"therefore all Teucria looses itself from its long grief;",
"and so all Troy shakes off its long grief;",
"'ergo' states the consequence. 'Teucria' is the land of the Teucri, the Trojans (named for King Teucer); the whole country is the subject. 'solvit se' — 'looses itself', like a prisoner released. 'longo luctu' — the long grief of the siege; the relief is brief, and Aeneas knows it."),
((27, 'portae;'),
"the gates are thrown open;",
"the gates are flung wide;",
"'panduntur' — the passive of 'pando', 'are spread open'."),
((28, 'relictum.'),
"it pleases to go, and to see the Doric camp\nand the abandoned places and the shore left behind.",
"it is a joy to walk out and see the Greek camp, the deserted ground, and the shore they left.",
"'iuvat ire… videre' — 'it pleases (us) to go… to see' (an impersonal verb with infinitives). 'Dorica' means 'Greek' by another synonym, from the Doric region of Greece. 'desertos… locos' — 'locos' is the masculine plural of 'locus' (the plural could be 'loci' or 'loca'). 'litus relictum' — the shore where their ships had been beached."),
((29, 'Achilles;'),
"Here the Dolopian band, here fierce Achilles pitched his tent;",
"Here the Dolopes were posted, here fierce Achilles had his quarters;",
"The Trojans walk through the camp pointing out where each contingent had been. 'tendebat' is used without an object, 'was encamped'. 'Dolopum' — old genitive plural; see line 7. 'saevus Achilles' — the standing epithet 'fierce' for the great enemy, dead by now. The repeated 'hic… hic' is the pointing of the sightseers."),
((30, 'locus;'),
"here was the place for the fleet;",
"here the ships had their place;",
"'classibus hic locus' — a dative of purpose or possession, 'the place for the fleets', with 'est' understood. The sentence is a series of short, verbless statements: the Trojans are telling each other the history of the ground."),
((30, 'solebant.'),
"here they used to fight in battle line.",
"here they used to meet us in battle.",
"'acie certare' — 'to contend with the battle line', ablative of means or manner; 'solebant' — the imperfect of habit. With this, Virgil closes the tour and returns to the horse."),
((32, 'equi;'),
"A part is amazed at the deadly gift of unwed Minerva,\nand they marvel at the bulk of the horse;",
"Some stand amazed at the deadly offering to the maiden Minerva, and marvel at the bulk of the horse;",
"'Pars stupet… mirantur' — a singular collective subject ('pars') with a plural verb in the next clause, the commonest kind of agreement by sense. 'innuptae… Minervae' — Minerva (Athena) is the unmarried goddess; the horse is supposed to be her gift. 'exitiale' — 'deadly', the sentence's secret: Aeneas, who is telling this afterwards, can say what the Trojans did not know. 'molem' is 'mass, bulk'."),
((34, 'ferebant.'),
"and first Thymoetes urges that it be led within the walls\nand placed in the citadel, whether by guile or because by now Troy's\nfates were bringing it so.",
"and Thymoetes is the first to urge that it be taken inside the walls and set up on the citadel, whether from treachery or because Troy's fate was already moving that way.",
"'primusque Thymoetes' — a Trojan elder; 'duci' and 'locari' are present passive infinitives after 'hortatur' ('urges that it be led… placed'), with the subject accusative 'equum' understood. 'arce' — 'in the citadel' (locative-like ablative). 'sive dolo, seu' — 'whether by guile or because…'; 'sive… seu' are two forms of the same conjunction, varied for style. 'fata ferebant' — the fates were bearing things that way: Aeneas, looking back, gives the whole catastrophe a cause that no human could have refused."),
((38, 'latebras.'),
"But Capys, and those whose mind has the better judgment,\neither into the sea the Danaans' ambush and suspect gifts\nbid them hurl, and by flames set beneath burn it,\nor bore through the hollow hiding places of the belly and probe them.",
"But Capys, and those with sounder minds, tell them either to throw the Greeks' trap and their suspect gift into the sea, or to burn it with fire set underneath, or to bore into the hollow hiding places of its belly and probe them.",
"'At' sets the opposing party against Thymoetes. 'quorum melior sententia menti' — 'whose mind had a better opinion', a dative of possession ('menti' is dative of 'mens'). The verb 'iubent' has a plural subject (Capys and those with him), and two pairs of infinitives hang on it: 'praecipitare' and 'urere' (the sea or fire), and 'terebrare' and 'temptare' (the third course). 'Danaum insidias' — 'Danaum' is the old genitive plural again. 'suspecta dona' — 'gifts suspected'. 'subiectis flammis' — ablative absolute, 'fire placed under'. 'uteri' is the genitive depending on 'latebras'."),
((39, 'volgus.'),
"The wavering crowd is split into opposite zeals.",
"The wavering crowd is torn between opposing wishes.",
"'Scinditur… volgus' — 'volgus' is Greenough's spelling of 'vulgus', the common people, a neuter noun (hence 'incertum', neuter). 'studia in contraria' — 'into opposite enthusiasms', the preposition showing the direction of the split. The line closes the debate with a very Roman image of a mob divided."),
((42, 'procul:'),
"First there, before all, with a great throng accompanying,\nLaocoön, blazing, runs down from the top of the citadel,\nand from far off:",
"Foremost among them, and at the head of a great crowd, Laocoön comes running hot-faced down from the top of the citadel, and cries from far off:",
"A new paragraph. Laocoön is a Trojan priest (Virgil says whose, later in the book); his is a Greek name, and Greenough keeps the diaeresis in 'Laocoön'. 'ante omnis' = 'ante omnes', before all. 'magna comitante caterva' — ablative absolute, 'a great crowd accompanying'. 'ardens' — burning with zeal. 'summa… ab arce' — 'from the highest part of the citadel'. 'et procul' — another dropped verb of speaking ('he cries out from afar'), the colon bringing in the speech. The speech itself is not opened by a verb; the mark follows."),
((42, 'cives?'),
"“O wretched ones, what so great madness, citizens?",
"“Wretched fools, what madness is this, fellow citizens?",
"'O miseri' — vocative plural: 'wretched people'. 'quae tanta insania' — 'what so great madness (is this)?', an exclamatory question with 'est' dropped. 'cives' is a vocative, in the same plural. The opening is the standard way of denouncing a folly. Speech marks as printed by Greenough: opening on this unit, closing at 49."),
((43, 'hostis?'),
"Do you believe the enemy sailed away?",
"Do you think the enemy have sailed off?",
"'Creditis' — a question with no particle, tone alone. 'avectos hostis' — 'hostis' is accusative plural (= 'hostes'); the accusative-and-infinitive 'avectos (esse)' follows 'creditis', so 'hostes' is subject of the infinitive: 'do you believe the enemy carried away?'. The sentence is a flat denial in the form of a question."),
((44, 'Danaum?'),
"Or do you think any gifts\nare free of Danaan guile?",
"Or do you suppose any gift of the Greeks is free of treachery?",
"'Aut ulla putatis' — the second member, 'or do you suppose that any…'. 'carere dolis' — 'to lack tricks', 'carere' takes the ablative. 'Danaum' is again the old genitive plural. 'Aut' opens the second question without any particle of doubt: the speaker is pressing, not asking."),
((44, 'Ulixes?'),
"Is Ulysses so known?",
"Is that how we know Ulysses?",
"'Sic notus Ulixes?' — a clipped, ironic question: 'is this how Ulysses is known to you?', or 'is that the Ulysses we know?' Laocoön appeals to his reputation for tricks. The 'sic' is 'in this way', and 'notus' (known) has 'est' dropped."),
((48, 'error;'),
"Either, shut in this wood, Achaeans are hidden,\nor this engine has been built against our walls,\nto look down on our houses, and to come down on the city from above,\nor some trick lies hidden;",
"Either Greeks lie hidden, shut up inside this timber, or this engine has been built to overlook our houses and come down on the city from above, or some other trick is lurking;",
"'aut… aut… aut' — three alternatives. 'inclusi ligno' — 'shut in the wood': 'ligno' is ablative, used for the wooden horse. 'Achivi' is another name for the Greeks. 'haec… machina' — 'machina' is a siege-engine (to climb the walls). 'inspectura' and 'ventura' are future participles used for purpose ('about to look into', 'about to come'); they describe what the machine is meant to do. 'urbi' is dative, 'against the city'. 'aliquis latet error' — 'some error (deceit) lies hidden'. The three guesses end with the vague 'some error': a speaker who does not know, and only knows he fears."),
((48, 'Teucri.'),
"do not trust the horse, Teucrians.",
"do not trust the horse, men of Troy.",
"'equo ne credite' — 'ne' with the imperative, the poetic form of a prohibition ('credite', imperative plural of 'credo', which takes the dative 'equo'). 'Teucri' is vocative plural, 'Trojans', after their ancestor Teucer."),
((49, 'ferentis.”'),
"Whatever it is, I fear the Danaans, and them bringing gifts.”",
"Whatever that thing may be, I fear the Greeks, and fear them with presents in their hands.”",
"The most-quoted line in the book, and it can be misread. 'Quicquid id est' — 'whatever it is' (Greenough prints 'Quicquid'; many editions print 'quidquid'). 'timeo Danaos et dona ferentis' — 'ferentis' = 'ferentes', a present participle in the accusative plural agreeing with 'Danaos'; 'et' means 'even, also' — 'the Greeks, and (them) even when they bring gifts'. Laocoön does not say 'beware the gifts' but 'I fear the men'. This rendering is built from the Latin alone and recorded in new-renderings.md. The closing speech mark ends the speech that began at 42."),
((52, 'contorsit:'),
"So having spoken, with strong strength a huge spear\ninto the flank and into the curved belly of the beast, with its joinings,\nhe hurled:",
"Having spoken so, he drove with all his strength a huge spear into the creature's side, into the curved belly with its jointed timbers, and hurled it:",
"'Sic fatus' — 'having spoken thus', a perfect participle of the deponent 'for' (to speak) used as a stock formula of the narrative. 'validis ingentem viribus hastam' — hyperbaton: the adjective 'validis' goes with 'viribus', 'ingentem' with 'hastam', and the two phrases interlock. 'feri' (genitive) is the horse, 'beast' (as a monster); 'compagibus' (ablative) is 'with its joints', the planks fitted together. The sentence runs through the lines and puts the verb 'contorsit' (he hurled) at the end, like the spear in flight."),
((53, 'cavernae.'),
"it stood, quivering, and, the womb shaken,\nthe hollow caverns resounded and gave a groan.",
"The spear stood quivering, and as the belly shook, the hollow chambers rang and gave out a groan.",
"'stetit illa tremens' — the spear stuck in the wood and shook. 'utero recusso' — ablative absolute, 'the belly having been shaken back' ('recutio', to strike back). 'insonuere' = 'insonuerunt', the -ere perfect again. 'gemitum dedere' = 'gemitum dederunt', 'gave a groan' (another -ere form): the horse answers with a human sound, from the men inside, though no one hears what it means."),
((56, 'maneres.'),
"And, if the fates of the gods, if the mind not been perverse,\nhe had urged us to defile with iron the Argive hiding places,\nand, Troy, you would now stand, and you, Priam's high citadel, would remain.",
"And if the fates of the gods, and our own minds, had not been against us, he would have driven us to rip open that Greek hiding place with the sword, and you, Troy, would still stand, and Priam's high citadel would still be here.",
"Aeneas, in hindsight, turns to address Troy itself ('Troia… stares'): an apostrophe. The sentence is a mixed conditional: 'si fata deum, si mens non laeva fuisset' (pluperfect subjunctive, 'if the fates had not… if the mind had not been wrong'), 'impulerat' (pluperfect indicative for 'impulisset'; the indicative makes the result vivid, 'he had all but driven us'), and 'stares… maneres' (imperfect subjunctive, 'you would now be standing'). 'deum' is the old genitive plural of 'deus' (= 'deorum'). 'laeva' — 'left', meaning ill-omened, wrong, since for Romans the left was often the unlucky side. 'Argolicas… latebras' — 'Argolic' is another way of saying Greek. 'foedare' — 'to defile', that is, to break open. Greenough prints 'stares' (second person, addressing Troy); the Latin Library has 'staret', which would make it a statement about Troy in the third person."),
((61, 'paratus,'),
"Behold, as to the hands, bound behind the back a young man, meanwhile,\nshepherds to the great king with great shouting were dragging,\nDardans, who, unknown, to those coming, of his own accord,\nin order to work this very thing and open Troy to the Achaeans,\nhad offered himself, confident in heart and ready for either course,",
"Meanwhile, look, Dardan shepherds were dragging before the king, with a great uproar, a young man whose hands were tied behind his back, who had put himself in the way of the newcomers, a stranger and of his own will, so as to bring this very thing about and open Troy to the Greeks, sure of his nerve and ready either way,",
"A new paragraph. 'Ecce' — 'look!', a sudden turn, the narrator's pointing. 'manus… revinctum' — 'bound as to the hands': 'manus' is an accusative of respect (a Greek-style construction) with the participle, and 'iuvenem' is the object of 'trahebant'; 'pastores' is the subject, 'Dardanidae' in apposition. 'Dardanidae' — 'sons of Dardanus', the Trojans. 'magno ad regem' — 'to the great king', Priam. 'qui se… obtulerat' — 'qui' refers to the young man; 'ignotum' agrees with 'se'. 'hoc ipsum ut strueret… aperiret' — purpose clauses. 'fidens animi' — a genitive of respect ('confident in spirit'). 'in utrumque paratus' — 'ready for either'. The long sentence is divided at the comma because it would exceed five lines; the unit ends at 'paratus,' and the last pair of alternatives forms the next unit."),
((62, 'morti.'),
"either to ply tricks or to fall to certain death.",
"either to carry through his tricks or to meet a sure death.",
"'seu versare dolos, seu… occumbere morti' — 'whether to turn over his tricks, or to fall to sure death'; the dative with 'occumbere' ('fall to death') is standard for this verb. 'certae… morti' — 'sure death', that is, death that cannot be avoided if his trick fails. The two alternatives are the two outcomes of Sinon's plan, to succeed or to die, and the young man is ready for both."),
((64, 'capto.'),
"From all sides in eagerness to see, the Trojan youth\npour round him, and vie to mock the captive.",
"From every side, eager to see him, the young men of Troy pour round and compete to jeer at their prisoner.",
"'visendi studio' — 'by eagerness of seeing': a gerund in the genitive. 'circumfusa' is a perfect participle, 'poured around'. 'ruit' — 'rushes', in the singular for the collective 'iuventus'; then 'certant' (plural). 'inludere capto' — 'to mock the captive', a dative after a compound verb with an infinitive."),
((66, 'omnes.'),
"Hear now the Danaans' ambushes, and from one crime\nlearn all.",
"Hear now the treacheries of the Greeks, and from one charge of crime learn what they all are.",
"Aeneas turns to Dido: 'Accipe' is addressed to her ('hear now'). 'crimine ab uno disce omnes' — a famous phrase: 'from one crime learn all', the particular standing for the whole. 'crimen' here means 'charge' or 'crime'. 'disce' — imperative. The line closes the paragraph, with the next beginning the story of Sinon."),
((68, 'circumspexit:'),
"For as, in the middle of the sight, confused, unarmed,\nhe stood, and with his eyes looked around the Phrygian lines:",
"For when he stood there in full view, bewildered and unarmed, and looked round with his eyes at the ranks of Phrygians:",
"A new paragraph. 'Namque ut' — 'for when', 'ut' with the perfect indicative ('constitit… circumspexit') means 'when, as soon as'. 'in medio conspectu' — in the open, in the sight of all. 'Phrygia' means Trojan (Phrygia is the region around Troy). 'circumspexit' — the perfect of 'circumspicio'. The colon brings in Sinon's speech."),
((70, 'accipere?'),
"“Alas, what land now, said he, what seas can\nreceive me?",
"“Alas, what land now, he said, what seas can take me in?",
"The speech begins with 'inquit' set into it, with no commas around it in Greenough's printing (as printed: 'Heu, quae nunc tellus inquit quae me aequora possunt'); the effect is a single breath of despair. 'Heu' — an interjection of grief. 'possunt accipere' — 'can receive'. The opening mark is on this unit."),
((72, 'poscunt?”'),
"Or what, now, is left to wretched me at last,\nwho have no place anywhere among the Danaans, and in addition the very\nDardans, hostile, demand my punishment with my blood?”",
"Or what, in the end, is left to a wretch like me, who have no place anywhere among the Greeks, and for whom the Trojans themselves, in their hatred, demand a penalty paid in blood?”",
"'quid… restat' — 'what is left?'. 'misero mihi' — dative of the person affected. 'cui' is a relative with a dative of possession: 'to whom there is no place'. 'neque apud Danaos usquam locus' — no place among the Greeks anywhere (with 'est' understood). 'et super' — 'and in addition' (the preposition used as an adverb). 'ipsi Dardanidae infensi' — 'the Dardans themselves, hostile'. 'poenas cum sanguine poscunt' — 'poenas poscere' means to demand punishment; 'cum sanguine' is 'with blood', that is, in blood. The closing mark ends this speech."),
((74, 'impetus.'),
"At that groan our minds were turned, and all\nthe onset was checked.",
"At that groan our hearts were moved, and all our fury was checked.",
"'Quo gemitu' — a connecting relative (= 'et eo gemitu'), 'at which groan' (a form you'll see constantly). 'conversi animi' — the minds are 'turned', changed from anger to pity. 'compressus' (est) is a perfect passive, 'was pressed down'. Aeneas now speaks as one of the Trojans ('we')."),
((74, 'fari;'),
"We urge him to speak;",
"We urge him to speak;",
"'Hortamur fari' — 'we urge (him) to speak'; 'fari' is the present infinitive of the deponent 'for'. The object 'eum' is dropped."),
((75, 'capto.'),
"from what blood sprung, or what he brings, let him tell,\nwhat confidence the captive has.",
"to tell what blood he is sprung from, what he has come to tell us, what confidence a prisoner has.",
"An indirect command / question hanging on 'hortamur': 'quo sanguine cretus' ('sprung from what blood'), 'quid ferat' ('what he brings'), 'memoret' (a jussive subjunctive, 'let him tell'), 'quae sit fiducia' ('what ground of confidence there is'). The three clauses without 'et' are asyndeton (a series with no connectives). 'cretus' is the participle of 'cresco', here 'born of'. 'capto' is dative: 'for a prisoner'."),
((76, 'fatur:]'),
"[He, these things, his fear at last laid aside, says:]",
"[Then, his fear at last put aside, he spoke as follows:]",
"A bracketed line: Greenough prints II.76 in square brackets as spurious (a line he judges not to be Virgil's). It is kept as its own unit and printed in brackets as in the edition; the Latin Library does not print it at all. It is only a stage direction, introducing Sinon's reply. 'Ille' is Sinon. 'deposita… formidine' — ablative absolute, 'fear laid aside'. 'fatur' is the historic present of the deponent 'for'."),
((78, 'inquit;'),
"“All things indeed to you, King, whatever it shall be, I will confess\ntrue, he says;",
"“I will tell you everything, King, whatever comes of it, and tell it true,",
"A new paragraph. 'Cuncta… vera… fatebor' — 'I will confess all things true', with 'vera' as predicate (the hyperbaton wraps the verb around the verse). 'equidem' — 'for my part, indeed'. 'Rex' is Priam. 'fuerit quodcumque' — future perfect, 'whatever it shall have been', 'whatever it turns out to be'. 'inquit' comes in the middle of the speech (as with 'inquit' on 69): the speech does not stop for the tag, and the mark stays on the first unit only. The unit ends at the semicolon after 'inquit'."),
((78, 'negabo:'),
"and I shall not deny that I am of Argive stock:",
"and I will not deny that I am of Argive stock:",
"'neque me Argolica de gente negabo' — 'I will not deny myself to be from the Argive race'. 'negabo' takes the accusative-and-infinitive, with 'me' as subject and 'esse' dropped. Sinon's strategy is to confess the true thing first, which is that he is a Greek."),
((79, 'primum;'),
"this first;",
"that first of all;",
"'hoc primum' — 'this first', the opening of an enumeration (the rest follows). The ellipsis of the verb is the speaker's rhetoric."),
((80, 'finget.'),
"nor, if Fortune has made wretched Sinon,\nwill she also make him vain and a liar, the wicked one.",
"and if Fortune has made Sinon wretched, she will not, wicked as she is, make him a liar and a cheat besides.",
"'Sinonem' is the first time the prisoner is named. 'nec, si… finxit, … finget' — a condition with a future-looking apodosis: 'if Fortune has made X, she will not make Y'. 'finxit… finget' — the same verb twice (a play on its two senses, 'shape' and 'invent'); in the second sense 'fingere' is also 'to feign'. 'improba' (nominative) agrees with 'Fortuna'. The irony, for the reader and for Aeneas telling it, is that Sinon is lying throughout. 'vanum… mendacemque' — 'vain' here means 'false'."),
((85, 'lugent.'),
"If by chance in telling any word has come to your ears\nof Palamedes the Belid's name, and his glory, famed by report,\nwhom, under a false charge of treason, the Pelasgians,\ninnocent, by an infamous accusation, because he opposed the war,\nsent down to death, now bereft of light they mourn.",
"If the name of Palamedes, Belus's descendant, has ever happened to reach your ears in the telling, and the glory of his renown, whom the Greeks, on a false charge of treason, sent innocent to his death by a wicked accusation because he spoke against the war, and now, with him dead, they mourn him.",
"'Fando' — ablative of the gerund, 'by telling', 'by hearsay'. 'si forte… pervenit' — 'if by chance… has reached', indicative. 'Belidae' — a patronymic: son (descendant) of Belus. Palamedes was a Greek hero; according to the later legend he exposed Ulysses's pretended madness to avoid the war, and Ulysses later had him framed as a traitor. The reader of Virgil's day knew the story, which is not in Homer. 'Pelasgi' — another name for the Greeks. 'falsa sub proditione' — 'under a false charge of treason'. 'insontem' — 'guiltless'. 'infando indicio' — an 'unspeakable' (wicked) information laid against him. 'demisere neci' = 'demiserunt neci' (the -ere perfect); 'neci' is a dative of 'nex'. 'cassum lumine' — 'deprived of light', a euphemism for dead. The conditional is never completed: Sinon breaks off the 'if' and goes straight into the story (anacoluthon)."),
((90, 'gessimus.'),
"To him, as companion and close in kinship,\nmy father, poor, to arms sent me hither from my first years,\nwhile he stood safe in his kingship and flourished in the\ncounsels of kings, and we also some name and glory\nbore.",
"To him, as his comrade and near by blood, my father, a poor man, sent me here to the war from my earliest years, while he stood unharmed in his rule and had weight in the councils of kings, and we too held some name and honour.",
"'Illi me comitem… misit' — the order is intentionally Latin: 'to him me as companion… sent'. 'consanguinitate propinquum' — 'close by blood', an ablative of respect. 'pauper' — the father is poor, so he could not afford to send his son as anything but a follower. 'in arma' — 'into war'. 'primis ab annis' — 'from the first years'. 'dum stabat… vigebat' — 'dum' with the imperfect indicative, 'while'. 'regno incolumis' — 'safe in his kingdom' (his standing among the Greek leaders). 'consiliis' — Greenough's spelling (the Latin Library has 'conciliis'; the sense is the same). 'nomen decusque gessimus' — 'we bore name and honour'. The tie of kinship is part of Sinon's story."),
((93, 'amici.'),
"After, through the envy of the wily Ulysses —\nI speak not unknown things — he departed from the upper shores,\nI, afflicted, dragged my life in darkness and grief,\nand over the fate of my innocent friend I raged with myself.",
"But after he withdrew from the world above, through the envy of crafty Ulysses (I speak of nothing unknown), I dragged out my life in gloom and mourning, cast down, and raged to myself at the fate of my innocent friend.",
"A parenthesis set off by dashes: 'haud ignota loquor' — 'I am saying nothing unknown', that is, 'you know this story'. 'Invidia postquam pellacis Ulixi' — 'Ulixi' is the old genitive of 'Ulixes'. 'superis concessit ab oris' — a euphemism: he 'withdrew from the upper shores', that is, he died. 'pellacis' — 'deceitful'. 'adflictus' — 'cast down', from 'affligo'. 'luctu' is ablative. 'casum insontis… amici' — 'the fall of my innocent friend'. 'mecum indignabar' — 'I was indignant with myself', 'I seethed inwardly'. Sinon sets himself up as the loyal friend of the victim."),
((96, 'movi.'),
"Nor did I keep silent, madly, and I myself, if any chance should bring it,\nif ever I should return victor to my native Argos,\nI promised an avenger, and with words I stirred bitter hatreds.",
"Nor did I keep quiet, like a madman: I promised to avenge him myself if any chance should bring it about, if ever I came home a victor to my native Argos, and with these words I stirred up bitter hatred.",
"'demens' — in apposition with the subject, 'madly, like a fool'. 'fors si qua tulisset' — a pluperfect subjunctive in an indirect conditional inside the reported promise: 'if any chance should have offered itself'. 'remeassem' = 'remeavissem'. 'victor' is a nominative, predicate. 'promisi ultorem' — 'I promised (myself as) an avenger'; the verb takes the accusative-and-infinitive, with 'me esse' dropped. 'verbis odia aspera movi' — 'I stirred sharp hatreds with words'. The speech of an inexperienced man, he says, who ruined himself by talking."),
((99, 'arma.'),
"From this the first stain of evil to me, from this ever Ulysses\nwith new accusations to terrify, from this to scatter ambiguous\nwords among the crowd, and to seek weapons, conscious.",
"From this came my first taint of misfortune: from this Ulysses was always frightening me with fresh charges, from this scattering ambiguous rumours among the people, and seeking arms, knowing what he did.",
"'Hinc… hinc… hinc' — three repeated 'from this', an anaphora. 'mali labes' — 'stain of evil'. The second and third clauses use historic infinitives ('terrere', 'spargere', 'quaerere'), which stand in for finite verbs in vivid narrative (in the imperfect sense, 'kept terrifying', 'kept spreading'); you'll see this repeatedly. 'in volgum' — 'volgum' is Greenough's spelling of the accusative of 'vulgus', here treated as masculine (at 39 it is neuter). 'ambiguas' — 'double-meaning'. 'conscius' — 'knowingly' (of Ulysses, who knows his own guilt). 'arma' — means, weapons, a figurative sense."),
((100, 'ministro—'),
"For he did not rest, until, Calchas as his instrument—",
"For he did not rest until, with Calchas as his tool—",
"'Nec requievit enim' — 'enim' here explains and carries on the story ('for he did not rest'). 'Calchante ministro' — ablative absolute: 'Calchas being the servant'. Calchas is the Greeks' seer, who (in Sinon's tale) was made to denounce Sinon. The dash is Sinon's break-off: the sentence is left unfinished, as he pretends not to be able to say it."),
((101, 'revolvo?'),
"but why do I, in vain, unwelcome, go over these things?",
"but why do I go back over these things, to no purpose, and unwelcome?",
"'sed quid' — 'but why?'. 'haec autem nequiquam ingrata revolvo' — 'autem' adds the turn; 'nequiquam' is 'in vain', 'ingrata' (neuter plural accusative, 'unwelcome things'). 'revolvo' — 'roll back'. The question is a stage-pause: Sinon pretends to see the uselessness of his story."),
((103, 'est?'),
"Or why do I delay, if you hold all the Achaeans in one rank,\nand it is enough to hear this?",
"Or why do I linger, if you reckon all Greeks as of one stamp, and it is enough to hear that?",
"'Quidve moror' — 'or why do I delay?' (the enclitic '-ve' = 'or'). 'si omnis uno ordine habetis Achivos' — 'omnis' = 'omnes' (the accusative plural in -is); 'uno ordine habetis' — 'you hold (them) in one class', 'you rank them all the same'. 'idque audire sat est' — 'sat' = 'satis'. This plays on the line 65–66 maxim 'disce omnes': the Trojans (Sinon suggests) already think all Greeks the same, so there is no need to tell more."),
((104, 'Atridae.”'),
"At once take your penalties,\nthe Ithacan would wish this, and the Atridae would buy it dear.”",
"Take your vengeance now, at once; that is what the Ithacan would want, and the sons of Atreus would pay a great price for it.”",
"'Iamdudum' — 'long since', 'this very moment'; 'sumite poenas' — 'exact punishment', imperative. 'Ithacus' — Ulysses, king of Ithaca. 'velit' — a potential subjunctive, 'would wish'. 'mercentur' — 'would buy', a present indicative as a vivid future. 'magno' — ablative of price ('at a great price'). 'Atridae' — Agamemnon and Menelaus, the sons of Atreus. The closing mark is Greenough's: Sinon's long speech, begun at 77, ends here."),
]
